"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_ANADOLU_EGE:-2169-01-01:0":{"b": ["End of Troy II / Troy III (Early Bronze Age): the Troy III phase ends", "1237db63"], "d": ["14C-AMS of animal bone from the Cincinnati excavation (26 samples), modelled with IntCal20: the Troy II/III transition 2267±10, the end of Troy III 2170±10 cal BC.", "b3af61be"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_ANADOLU_EGE:-2266-01-01:0":{"b": ["End of Troy II / Troy III (Early Bronze Age): the Troy III phase begins", "0e6da4ee"], "d": ["14C-AMS of animal bone from the Cincinnati excavation (26 samples), modelled with IntCal20: the Troy II/III transition 2267±10, the end of Troy III 2170±10 cal BC.", "b3af61be"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_ANADOLU_EGE:-2919-01-01:0":{"b": ["Troy I (Hisarlik, beginning of the Early Bronze Age): the Troy Ia phase begins (Weninger 1995)", "2d436c8d"], "d": ["A date derived from dendro-calibrated 14C for the beginning of the Troy Ia phase (apsidal stone foundation); Troy I comprises 9 architectural phases in total (Ia-Ii), assuming an average phase length of 50 years.", "2f40873c"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_ANADOLU_EGE:-9149-01-01:0":{"b": ["Klimonas and Shillourokambos (Cyprus, Early Aceramic Neolithic): the first sedentary villages on Cyprus (Klimonas)", "7f9f9d76"], "d": ["The villagers established at Klimonas lived 11,100-10,600 years ago (highest probability 10.8-10.6 cal kyBP). Shillourokambos Early A 10.4-10.0, Early B-C 10.0-9.6, Middle A ~9.5 cal kyBP (caption of Fig. 3, comparison).", "c01f6b39"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0183-01-01:0":{"b": ["Maurya Empire: the Maurya dynasty ends", "1b4bed2f"], "d": ["Chandragupta (c. 321–297 BC), Bindusara (25/27/28 years), Ashoka; the dynasty ends in c. 184 BC with the killing of Brihadratha by Pushyamitra, according to the Puranic total of 137 years (Shunga period).", "5c9e3720"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0201-01-01:0":{"b": ["Founding of the Han dynasty — Liu Bang's proclamation as emperor (202 BC): the Western Han dynasty is founded", "b6d66651"], "d": ["After the Chu–Han war, Liu Bang took the imperial title in the 5th year of Han; the Western Han period is counted from 202.", "c15c87db"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0236-01-01:0":{"b": ["The reign of Ashoka and the synchronism of Rock Edict XIII: Ashoka dies", "39f8eb23"], "d": ["The reign of Ashoka: according to CHI 1922, accession ≤274, coronation ≤270, conquest of Kalinga ≤262, death 237/236 BC. Rock Edict XIII names five Hellenistic kings as living (Antiochus, Ptolemy, Antigonus, Magas, Alexander); because of Magas's death in 258, CHI provisionally places the edict at 'about 258–257 BC'.", "62399b89"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0273-01-01:0":{"b": ["The reign of Ashoka and the synchronism of Rock Edict XIII: Ashoka ascends the throne", "7a0e6e1c"], "d": ["The reign of Ashoka: according to CHI 1922, accession ≤274, coronation ≤270, conquest of Kalinga ≤262, death 237/236 BC. Rock Edict XIII names five Hellenistic kings as living (Antiochus, Ptolemy, Antigonus, Magas, Alexander); because of Magas's death in 258, CHI provisionally places the edict at 'about 258–257 BC'.", "62399b89"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0320-01-01:0":{"b": ["Maurya Empire: Maurya Empire: the Chandragupta period begins", "43e81ed7"], "d": ["Chandragupta (c. 321–297 BC), Bindusara (25/27/28 years), Ashoka; the dynasty ends in c. 184 BC with the killing of Brihadratha by Pushyamitra, according to the Puranic total of 137 years (Shunga period).", "5c9e3720"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0325-01-01:0":{"b": ["Nanda dynasty (Magadha): Dhana Nanda in Magadha (age of Alexander)", "64e72b4d"], "d": ["The nine Nandas (Mahapadma and eight sons) in Magadha; the last (Dhana Nanda) was on the throne in 326 BC; in ~321 BC sovereignty passed to the Maurya. The date of the founding is not given in the source.", "c369d6b6"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0480-01-01:0":{"b": ["Beginning of the Chunqiu (Spring and Autumn Annals) record: the Chunqiu period ends", "aa4943a9"], "d": ["The Chunqiu annals of the state of Lu begin in the 1st year of Duke Yin (722) and end in the 14th year of Duke Ai (481); the name 'Spring and Autumn period' is based on this text.", "9abe05ef"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0482-01-01:0":{"b": ["Date of the Buddha's parinirvana (chronological anchor): the Buddha's parinirvana (Cambridge History date)", "e4107ac0"], "d": ["The Cambridge History of India (1922) places the Buddha's death in about 483 BCE by adding the 218-year interval found in the Ceylonese chronicles to Ashoka, and regards this as 'provisional'.", "0b992e60"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0542-01-01:0":{"b": ["Rise of Magadha (Bimbisara and Ajatashatru): Bimbisara ascends the throne of Magadha", "42ca9a86"], "d": ["The Cambridge History of India (1922), following the Geiger chronology based on the Sinhalese chronicles, gives the reigns of Bimbisara as 543-491 BCE and of Ajatashatru as 491-459 BCE; the rise of Magadha begins with the conquest of Anga (Bimbisara, c. 600 BCE), and the supremacy over Kashi-Kosala-Videha is attributed to Ajatashatru.", "c1adf1d6"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-0721-01-01:0":{"b": ["Beginning of the Chunqiu (Spring and Autumn Annals) record: the Chunqiu period begins", "a575d11c"], "d": ["The Chunqiu annals of the state of Lu begin in the 1st year of Duke Yin (722) and end in the 14th year of Duke Ai (481); the name 'Spring and Autumn period' is based on this text.", "9abe05ef"]},
"OLAYLAR_ARKEOLOJI_D3_HINT_CIN:-1045-01-01:0":{"b": ["Zhou conquest of the Shang (Muye) - date discrepancy: Zhou defeats the Shang (1046; sources say 1045/1044/1040/1050)", "2fb1528d"], "d": ["King Wu's victory over the Shang (Muye) and the founding of the Zhou dynasty; in the peer-reviewed literature the date is disputed among 1050/1046/1045/1044/1040.", "3cee8749"]},
